erally puts its **annual revenue somewhere in the low single‑digit millions**, consistent with a **rough \\$2–\\$7M/year** range. Employee data from LinkedIn and company profiles suggest a **team size in the mid‑teens** rather than just a handful of people.\n\nBelow I separate **confirmed facts** (from Brain.fm or primary sources) from **third‑party estimates** (Crunchbase‑style databases, revenue scrapers, etc.) and note when sources conflict.\n\n---\n\n## 1) Funding history — total venture/seed funding\n\n### Confirmed facts\n\n- Brain.fm states on its own site that it is **funded by a grant from the National Science Foundation (NSF‑STTR #1720698)**, indicating at least one NSF grant is a core funding source.[38]  \n- An AMA with the CEO on Reddit links directly to the NSF award page for **NSF STTR Phase I grant 1720698**, describing this as an **STTR Phase I** grant and quoting NSF guidance that such awards “normally do not exceed **\\$150,000 total costs for 1 year**.”[41]  \n  - This strongly supports a **Phase I grant of about \\$150k** in 2018 as confirmed public funding.\n- A Yahoo Finance news release on Brain.fm’s ADHD research states that their work is “**funded in part by the U.S. National Science Foundation**.”[45] This confirms NSF support but not the exact dollar amount beyond the Phase I figure above.\n\nThese are the only **primary, clearly confirmed numbers**: an NSF STTR Phase I grant around **\\$150k** and the fact that NSF funding is central.\n\n### Third‑party funding databases and secondary sources (estimates / partially documented)\n\nThese sites compile funding data from filings, NSF databases, and private reports; they sometimes disagree on totals.\n\n1. **Oryndex company profile (funding timeline)**  \n   - States **“Total Raised: \\$325K”** for Brain.fm.[31]  \n   - Funding timeline:  \n     - **Seed: \\$100K** (June 2016) — “initial capital was a small \\$100,000 seed investment from its co‑founders Adam Hewett and Junaid Kalmadi.”[31]  \n     - **Grant: \\$225K** (March 2018) — “their most significant capital infusion came from a \\$225,000 grant from the National Science Foundation (NSF) in 2018.”[31]  \n   - This implies total external + founder capital of **\\$325k**, with **\\$225k** being NSF grant money and **\\$100k** founder seed.  \n   - Classification: **Derived/compiled estimate** using NSF and other data — not a direct Brain.fm statement.\n\n2. **Caplight funding overview**  \n   - Lists **“Total Funding Raised: \\$325,000”**; last round: **Grant, Mar 20, 2018**.[33]  \n   - Confirms the same **\\$325k** total as Oryndex, but the grant line omits a specific amount in the visible table.[33]  \n   - Classification: **Aggregated funding database**, consistent with Oryndex’s total.\n\n3. **PitchBook company profile**  \n   - Shows a deal history table including:  \n     - **Capitalization: \\$100K on Jan 1, 2013** (startup stage).[32]  \n     - **Seed Round: 12 Apr 2017** (amount not clearly shown in the snippet).[32]  \n     - **Grant: \\$225K on 20 Mar 2018** (latest deal type: Grant, latest deal amount: \\$225K).[32]  \n   - The same page also includes a contradictory line: “**Brain.fm has raised \\$125K**” as total raised, despite showing \\$100k capitalization + \\$225k grant in the table.[32]  \n   - Classification: **Partially conflicting database** — the **\\$225k grant** is well supported; the **\\$125k total** is clearly inconsistent with its own table and with Oryndex/Caplight.\n\n4. **TheCompanyCheck profile**  \n   - Lists “**Latest Funding Round: \\$225K (USD), Grant, Mar 20, 2018**” and indicates **1 round** with a **total funding of \\$225K**.[39]  \n   - This supports at least **\\$225k** NSF grant funding, but ignores founder capitalization or any seed round.  \n   - Classification: **Partial record** – captures the grant, not the seed/founder capital.\n\n5. **Tracxn profile**  \n   - States **“Brain.fm has raised funding over 1 round… Grant (prize money), Mar 20, 2018, \\$***** … Brain.fm is a funded company, having raised a total of \\$***** in its 1st and only funding round”**.[37]  \n   - The numeric amounts are obfuscated in the visible portion (represented as placeholders), but this confirms there is at least one **grant round on 20 Mar 2018** from NSF.[37]  \n   - Classification: **Round confirmed, amount masked** in the snippet.\n\n6. **Owler funding page**  \n   - Lists 2 rounds (Grant Mar 2018 and Seed Apr 2017) but shows **“In total Brain.FM has raised \\$0”** and round amounts as “--”.[34]  \n   - This is clearly a **data error or placeholder** rather than a real figure.\n\n7. **Gaebler profile**  \n   - Shows a “**Last Transaction 5/31/2017**” line and a “Financing History” with **“Online Capital — \\$25,000”** together with mention of investor **Othman Laraki**.[35]  \n   - This suggests some kind of **\\$25k financing event** around 2017, possibly a small bridge or online funding product.[35]  \n   - Classification: **Secondary data**; the amount is specific but not cross‑validated elsewhere in the search results.\n\n8. **Aiifi AI tools directory**  \n   - Notes “**Undisclosed seed round raised Apr 12, 2017**.”[44]  \n   - No amount given; consistent only with the existence of a seed round on that date.\n\n9. **PitchBook competitor listing (WiredVibe AI page)**  \n   - Lists Brain.fm as **“Venture Capital‑Backed”** but does not show amounts in the snippet.[43]  \n   - Confirms presence of at least one VC investor (PitchBook earlier mentions **Slow Ventures** as a minority investor).[32][43]  \n   - Classification: **Status indication**, not a funding total.\n\n### Reconciling the numbers\n\n- **Minimum clearly documented external funding**:  \n  - NSF STTR Phase I grant: **≈\\$150k** (from NSF’s own program description and AMA citation).[41]  \n  - Third‑party databases and company‑profilers converge around **\\$225k** as the NSF grant figure.[31][32][39]  \n- **Founder / seed capital**:  \n  - Oryndex explicitly describes **\\$100k seed from co‑founders** in 2016.[31]  \n  - PitchBook lists a **\\$100k capitalization** in 2013, which may be the same or an earlier internal capitalisation.[32]  \n  - Aiifi notes an **undisclosed seed round on Apr 12, 2017**, and PitchBook mentions a 2017 seed round with **Slow Ventures** as an investor.[32][44]  \n- **Additional small financing** (less solid):  \n  - Gaebler reports a **\\$25k Online Capital transaction** in May 2017 and mentions investor **Othman Laraki**.[35]\n\nGiven these:\n\n- The **most consistent third‑party totals** (Oryndex + Caplight) are **\\$325k raised**:  \n  - **\\$225k NSF grant** (2018)  \n  - **\\$100k seed/founder capital** (2016/2013 capitalization)[31][33]  \n- PitchBook’s **“\\$125k total raised”** line contradicts its own deal table and is likely an error or partial tally.[32]  \n- There is **no credible source** in these results showing total funding anywhere near **\\$1M**, nor any substantial Series A/B venture rounds.\n\n### Direct answer to your funding question\n\n- **Is it true they raised only about \\$1M total?**  \n  Based on the available data, **no**. All credible aggregators put total funding closer to **\\$225k–\\$325k**, with one outlier (PitchBook text) incorrectly reporting **\\$125k** despite listing a \\$225k grant.[31][32][33][39]  \n- **Known/estimated rounds (with year, amount, source)**\n\n| Year / date      | Round / type                | Amount (confirmed/estimated) | Primary sources/notes |\n|------------------|----------------------------|------------------------------|------------------------|\n| Jan 1, 2013      | Capitalization (startup)   | **\\$100k** (internal capital) | PitchBook deal table shows \\$100k capitalization.[32] |\n| Jun 2016         | Seed / founder investment  | **\\$100k** (from co‑founders) | Oryndex describes \\$100k seed from Adam Hewett & Junaid Kalmadi; may overlap with 2013 capitalization.[31] |\n| Apr 12, 2017     | Seed round (VC‑backed)     | **Undisclosed** (possibly >\\$0) | Aiifi notes “undisclosed seed round”; PitchBook/Tracxn list a seed round with Slow Ventures, but snippet doesn’t show amount.[32][44] |\n| May 31, 2017     | Online Capital transaction | **\\$25k**                     | Gaebler lists “Online Capital — \\$25,000” and investor Othman Laraki.[35] |\n| Mar 20, 2018     | NSF STTR grant (Phase I)   | **\\$150k–\\$225k**            | Reddit AMA & NSF program guidance indicate ≈\\$150k; Oryndex, PitchBook, TheCompanyCheck all list \\$225k.[31][32][39][41] |\n\n**Best evidence total**: approximately **\\$225k–\\$325k**, far below \\$1M. The explicit claim that Brain.fm “raised only about \\$1M total” is **not supported**; instead, evidence points to a **much smaller, grant‑heavy funding history**.\n\n---\n\n## 2) Current employee headcount\n\n### Company and LinkedIn statements\n\n- **Brain.fm official “About” page** lists its **team members by name and role**, showing at least ~13 individuals, including CEO, Director of Science, Director of Growth, engineering, operations, and multiple user success roles.[8]  \n  - This is not a declared “employee count” but implies a **double‑digit team** in practice.\n\n- **LinkedIn company page for brain.fm** shows the company as **“Privately Held … 11–50 employees”**, which is LinkedIn’s standard employee‑count band.[1]  \n  - This band confirms they are not a solo or micro‑team, but it does not give an exact figure.\n\n### Third‑party employee estimates (all are estimates)\n\n1. **Tracxn**  \n   - States: “**Brain.fm has 15 employees as of May 31, 2026**,” and repeats “**Brain.fm has 15 employees as of May 26**.”[4]  \n   - This is the **most precise and recent numeric estimate** in the search results.\n\n2. **PitchBook**  \n   - Brain.fm profile lists **“Employees: 13 total employees.”**[5]  \n   - Likely based on earlier data; shows a similar magnitude to Tracxn.\n\n3. **Growjo**  \n   - Brain.fm page: “Brain.fm has **14 Employees**.”[10]  \n   - Also gives a revenue‑per‑employee figure, but 14 is the key for headcount.\n\n4. **ZoomInfo**  \n   - Company overview: “The Brain.fm corporate office … has **19 employees**,” broken down by department.[11]  \n   - ZoomInfo often over‑counts contractors and historical staff, so this may be on the high side.\n\n5. **Crustdata**  \n   - Shows **“Employees: 12”** for brain.fm.[3]  \n   - Again, consistent with a mid‑teens team size.\n\n6. **SignalHire / other directories**  \n   - SignalHire and similar sources show an **employee range of 10–50** or “1–25” but not a precise figure.[15]  \n   - These ranges are generic, but again consistent with a small but not tiny company.\n\n### Direct answer on headcount\n\n- **Is it around 15 people?**  \n  Yes, that is well supported. Recent third‑party company‑data providers converge around **12–15 employees**, with Tracxn explicitly stating **15 employees as of late May 2026**.[3][4][5][10]  \n- **LinkedIn range**:  \n  - LinkedIn shows **“11–50 employees”**, which is a **range band**, not a precise count.[1]  \n- **Reasonable current estimate**:  \n  - Taking the official team page (≈13 named individuals)[8] and the latest numeric data (12–15, Tracxn/Growjo/PitchBook/Crustdata), a **current headcount of about 13–15 employees** is the most defensible range.  \n  - ZoomInfo’s 19‑employee figure is an upper bound that may include non‑current staff or contractors.[11]\n\nSo the claim that Brain.fm has “about 15 people” is **well aligned** with these sources; the LinkedIn data supports only that they are in the **11–50 band**, but multiple independent datasets point to **mid‑teens** as the best estimate.\n\n---\n\n## 3) Annual revenue estimates (Growjo, ZoomInfo, RocketReach, Sensor Tower, etc.)\n\nBrain.fm does not publicly disclose precise revenue in the material surfaced, so all concrete numbers here are **third‑party estimates**. I’ll list each source and its figure, then assess whether they support the **\\$2M–\\$7M/year** range.\n\n### Third‑party revenue estimates\n\n1. **Growjo**  \n   - Brain.fm profile: “Brain.fm’s **estimated annual revenue is currently \\$2.6M per year**,” and “estimated revenue per employee is \\$186,429.”[16]  \n   - Classification: **Model‑based estimate**, typical Growjo methodology; not confirmed by Brain.fm.\n\n2. **ZoomInfo**  \n   - Brain.fm “Overview” page lists **“Revenue \\$7.1 Million”** for The Brain.fm.[17]  \n   - Classification: **Directory estimate**; ZoomInfo often uses ranges and modeled data, not company filings in cases like this.\n\n3. **RocketReach**  \n   - Brain.fm profile: “Revenue **\\$7.1 million**” and “The brain.fm annual revenue was **\\$7.1 million in 2024**.”[20]  \n   - This matches ZoomInfo’s figure, suggesting they may share a data source or model.\n\n4. **Owler**  \n   - Brain.fm company profile: “Est. **Annual Revenue \\$100K–5.0M**,” and a separate note that Brain.FM generates \\$316.7K revenue per employee.[24]  \n   - The wide range **\\$100k–\\$5M** is clearly approximate, but it **overlaps** the lower part of the \\$2M–\\$7M band.\n\n5. **AppRank / app‑revenue estimator (similar to Sensor Tower)**  \n   - A Brain.fm app revenue page reports:  \n     - **Monthly revenue \\$200.0K**  \n     - **Yearly revenue \\$2.4M**  \n     - Plus in‑app purchase price points for Brain.fm PRO subscriptions.[29]  \n   - This appears to be an **in‑app revenue estimate**, not necessarily total company revenue (Brain.fm also sells web subscriptions). But it directly supports at least **low‑single‑digit millions per year**.\n\n6. **PitchBook**  \n   - The Brain.fm profile has a Q&A section “What is Brain.fm’s current revenue?” but the snippet we saw does not include the numeric answer.[21]  \n   - So we cannot rely on a number here without the full entry.\n\n7. **Other sources (GetLatka, etc.)**  \n   - The Latka site snippet we have is for **The Brain** (a different company: personal knowledge software), not Brain.fm, with a **\\$389.8k** estimate.[23] That should not be applied to Brain.fm.  \n   - The general GetLatka landing page notes its numbers are CEO‑reported ARR for featured companies, but Brain.fm is not visible in the retrieved snippet.[28]\n\n### How these estimates compare\n\n- **Lower bound evidence**:  \n  - AppRank / app‑revenue style tool gives **\\$2.4M/year** from mobile app alone.[29]  \n  - Owler range **\\$100k–\\$5M** includes this level but is too broad to be precise.[24]\n\n- **Mid‑range evidence**:  \n  - Growjo’s model estimate is **\\$2.6M/year**.[16]  \n  - This is close to the AppRank figure and within Owler’s band.\n\n- **Upper bound evidence**:  \n  - ZoomInfo: **\\$7.1M revenue**.[17]  \n  - RocketReach: **\\$7.1M annual revenue in 2024**.[20]  \n  - These align almost exactly, suggesting a shared underlying dataset; they define a **high single‑digit revenue estimate**.\n\nBecause all of these are **estimates**, they should be treated as a **range** rather than precise truth. None of the surfaced sources provide audited financial statements or explicit Brain.fm disclosures. Still:\n\n- Multiple independent tools (Growjo, AppRank, Owler) point toward **low‑single‑digit millions**.\n- Directory estimates (ZoomInfo, RocketReach) suggest **around \\$7M**.\n\n### Direct answer to the revenue‑range question\n\n- **Claimed range: roughly \\$2M–\\$7M/year after ~10 years of operation. Does evidence support this?**  \n  Yes, the **third‑party estimates collectively support a range between about \\$2.4M and \\$7.1M per year**:\n\n  - **Lower/mid estimates**:  \n    - Growjo: **\\$2.6M**.[16]  \n    - AppRank: **\\$2.4M** (app revenue).[29]  \n  - **Upper estimates**:  \n    - ZoomInfo: **\\$7.1M**.[17]  \n    - RocketReach: **\\$7.1M (2024)**.[20]  \n  - **Range source**:  \n    - Owler: **\\$100k–\\$5M** (supports at least up to \\$5M).[24]\n\n  Given that app‑only revenue is estimated at \\$2.4M and broader company revenue is estimated as high as \\$7.1M, a **working range of roughly \\$2–\\$7M/year** is **consistent** with available data, with the **strongest cluster** of estimates in the **\\$2–\\$3M** zone and directory‑style tools pushing the top toward **\\$7M**.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of “confirmed facts” vs “estimates”\n\n- **Confirmed / primary facts**  \n  - Brain.fm is funded by at least one **NSF STTR grant (Phase I, award 1720698)**; Phase I grants typically are **≤\\$150k**, and multiple third‑party sources list **\\$225k** as the grant amount.[38][41][31][32][39]  \n  - Brain.fm’s own site lists a team of **≈13 named staff**, clearly indicating a **double‑digit team size**.[8]\n\n- **Best supported quantitative estimates**  \n  - **Total funding**: Most consistent aggregators (Oryndex, Caplight) show **\\$325k total** (founder capitalization + NSF grant).[31][33]  \n    - This is **far below \\$1M**; no credible source points to \\$1M+.  \n  - **Headcount**: Multiple independent data providers show **12–15 employees**, with Tracxn specifically noting **15 employees as of May 2026**.[3][4][5][10]  \n  - **Annual revenue**:  \n    - **Lower–mid band** around **\\$2.4–\\$2.6M** (Growjo, app‑revenue tools).[16][29]  \n    - **Upper band** around **\\$7.1M** (ZoomInfo, RocketReach).[17][20]  \n    - Owler’s **\\$100k–\\$5M** range is broadly consistent but non‑specific.[24]\n\nWithin the constraints of third‑party data, the most defensible picture is: **small grant‑backed company (~\\$300k total funding), mid‑teens headcount, low‑single‑digit‑million annual revenue, plausibly in the \\$2–\\$7M band.**","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/company/brain.fm","title":"brain.fm","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/danclark-brainfm","title":"Dan Clark - CEO at Brain.fm","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://crustdata.com/profiles/company/brainfm","t